wer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3f47e10-181c-587e-a0be-2b1e263782c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161646" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n- **I.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\,0<y<|2x|}$\n- **II.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- **III.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "103728c3-da2e-5746-827f-d6cd0408ce21"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c6d476-7089-554f-a53f-d0eeffbcff46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465435} {:answer/id #uuid "e2df68f7-1103-5962-9b9f-e16827511e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465436} :answer-field/id #uuid "349c0ef9-a03e-5c46-a30f-56ab22eb74e8" :answer-field/key 